[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Read report by report, it holds
three problems. The queued parser fix closes one of them. The other two keep coming in after it ships.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table counts by tenant and form. Read with the note under it, it holds three problems:

| problem | where it is in the table | ticket |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first | the `parser` row, less the one-day moves in the note | [#1](https://github.com/chillleader/mega-s01e03/issues/1) |
| `hint`: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field | the `hint` row, marked "no ticket of its own" | [#2](https://github.com/chillleader/mega-s01e03/issues/2) |
| `shift`: invites land one day early | the one-day moves in the note | [#3](https://github.com/chillleader/mega-s01e03/issues/3) |

## How each report was placed

1. **Repeats out first.** The ticket export comes in overlapping pages, so one id can appear twice. One id, one report.
2. **By tenant, not by intake tag.** Every tenant on file writes the day first. A report that came through the
   booking form of a `booking-v3` tenant is `hint`: that form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Every other
   report is `parser`. The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and disagrees often.
3. **One-day moves apart.** The desk already read some reports as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`): Tuesday shown
   as Monday, 12 May as 11 May, one meeting on two days for Lisbon and Toronto. Day and month are not swapped there,
   so the parser cannot cause it. The table files them under `parser` by tenant; they are their own problem.

## Why it matters

- **parser** is the fix already queued. Ship it.
- **hint** survives that fix: the form tells people to type month first and then reads the date day first. Change the
  hint on `booking-v3` to the tenant's order.
- **shift** survives both: the dates are read right and still land a day early. Look at time zones and DST where an
  invite's date is turned into a timestamp.

## Lessons

- Matching on subject merged three causes into one ticket ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  A count of reports says nothing about how many problems they hold.
- The desk saw the one-day moves and wrote them down, then filed them where the tenant pointed. A note that disagrees
  with the cause is a new problem, not a footnote.

